<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof sketch for Theorem 1 (idealized continuous-time model)</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix provides a proof sketch for the idealized continuous-time formulation in Section 3 (Kushner–Stratonovich filtering and HJB-motivated optimal stopping).
It does not apply directly to the discrete EMDG controller, which uses rollout-based VOI estimation and a myopic net-utility stopping rule (Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Discrete-time instantiation used in EMDG (rollout VOI + myopic stopping) ‣ 3 Formalizing Cognitive Friction: The Coupled Stochastic Control Objective ‣ The Triadic Cognitive Architecture: Bounding Autonomous Action via Spatio-Temporal and Epistemic Friction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We restate the core claim for the idealized model: under standard regularity conditions, the triadic cognitive control problem admits an optimal stopping time characterized by an HJB free boundary.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 1: Regularity of the filtering dynamics and Markov state.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let the observation process be governed by</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dY_{t}=h(\theta,\gamma_{t})\,dt+\sigma(\gamma_{t})\,dW_{t}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dY_{t}=h(\theta,\gamma_{t})\,dt+\sigma(\gamma_{t})\,dW_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\theta,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\theta,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy uniform Lipschitz continuity and linear growth bounds. Assume uniform non-degeneracy: there exists <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi^{\top}(\sigma\sigma^{\top})\xi\geq\epsilon\|\xi\|^{2}\qquad\forall\,\xi\in\mathbb{R}^{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^{\top}(\sigma\sigma^{\top})\xi\geq\epsilon\|\xi\|^{2}\qquad\forall\,\xi\in\mathbb{R}^{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Under these assumptions, the innovation process <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\nu_{t}=dY_{t}-\bar{h}(\gamma_{t})\,dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\nu_{t}=dY_{t}-\bar{h}(\gamma_{t})\,dt</annotation></semantics></math> is a Brownian motion with respect to the observation filtration <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the associated nonlinear filter (Kushner–Stratonovich; schematic form) is well-posed; see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. In particular, the controlled state <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{t},\gamma_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{t},\gamma_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is Markov with respect to <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}^{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>Y</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}^{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{h}(\gamma_{t})=\int_{\Theta}h(\theta,\gamma_{t})p_{t}(\theta)\,d\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{h}(\gamma_{t})=\int_{\Theta}h(\theta,\gamma_{t})p_{t}(\theta)\,d\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and the innovation</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\nu_{t}=dY_{t}-\bar{h}(\gamma_{t})\,dt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\nu_{t}=dY_{t}-\bar{h}(\gamma_{t})\,dt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">A schematic form of the Kushner–Stratonovich update is</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dp_{t}(\theta)=p_{t}(\theta)\big(h(\theta,\gamma_{t})-\bar{h}(\gamma_{t})\big)^{\top}(\sigma\sigma^{\top})^{-1}\,d\nu_{t}\quad(\text{omitting drift/normalization terms; see }\cite[cite]{\@@bibref{Authors Phrase1YearPhrase2}{kushner1964differential,bain2009fundamentals}{\@@citephrase{(}}{\@@citephrase{)}}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>omitting drift/normalization terms; see </mtext><mtext class="ltx_citemacro_cite"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite></mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dp_{t}(\theta)=p_{t}(\theta)\big(h(\theta,\gamma_{t})-\bar{h}(\gamma_{t})\big)^{\top}(\sigma\sigma^{\top})^{-1}\,d\nu_{t}\quad(\text{omitting drift/normalization terms; see }\cite[cite]{\@@bibref{Authors Phrase1YearPhrase2}{kushner1964differential,bain2009fundamentals}{\@@citephrase{(}}{\@@citephrase{)}}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 2: Value function and dynamic programming.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The agent seeks to maximize expected epistemic gain minus cognitive friction. For a stopping time <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\geq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\geq t</annotation></semantics></math>, write</p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\tau)=\mathbb{E}\left[\int_{t}^{\tau}f(p_{s},\gamma_{s},s)\,ds\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\tau)=\mathbb{E}\left[\int_{t}^{\tau}f(p_{s},\gamma_{s},s)\,ds\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(p,\gamma,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(p,\gamma,t)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the instantaneous net reward (information surrogate minus routing cost and temporal penalty), consistent with Section 3.
Let</p>
<table id="A1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(p,\gamma,t)=\sup_{\tau\geq t}J(\tau)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(p,\gamma,t)=\sup_{\tau\geq t}J(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">denote the value function. Assuming sufficient regularity of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> (or working in the viscosity-solution framework), standard dynamic programming arguments for controlled Markov processes yield a variational inequality characterization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Step 3: HJB variational inequality and the free boundary.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By optimal stopping theory, the value function satisfies a variational inequality of the form</p>
<table id="A1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\Big\{\partial_{t}V(p,\gamma,t)+\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\big[(\mathcal{L}^{u})V(p,\gamma,t)+f(p,\gamma,t;u)\big],\;-V(p,\gamma,t)\Big\}=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\Big\{\partial_{t}V(p,\gamma,t)+\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}}\big[(\mathcal{L}^{u})V(p,\gamma,t)+f(p,\gamma,t;u)\big],\;-V(p,\gamma,t)\Big\}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">in an appropriate classical or viscosity sense <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, where <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}^{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>u</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}^{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is the controlled infinitesimal generator for the Markov state <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{t},\gamma_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{t},\gamma_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as in Section 3.
Because <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous and bounded under the assumed cost structure, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous under standard assumptions.
Define the stopping region as the closed set</p>
<table id="A1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{(p,\gamma,t):V(p,\gamma,t)=0\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{(p,\gamma,t):V(p,\gamma,t)=0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and the continuation region as the open set</p>
<table id="A1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{(p,\gamma,t):V(p,\gamma,t)&gt;0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{(p,\gamma,t):V(p,\gamma,t)&gt;0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Then the first hitting time of the stopping region,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}=\inf\{s\geq t:(p_{s},\gamma_{s},s)\in D\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}=\inf\{s\geq t:(p_{s},\gamma_{s},s)\in D\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">is an optimal stopping time; moreover, it is the smallest (minimal) optimal stopping time in the usual sense for such problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. This completes the proof sketch.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
